00:00:03.439 --> 00:00:04.150 align:start position:0%
 
hello  i am thorsten whitman from the  department of cell and tissue biology  at the university of california in san  francisco  today i would like to tell you about our  recent work  on using optogenetics to locally and  acutely control microtubule dynamics in  life cells  to ask questions about how the  microtubule cytoskeleton  shapes cell morphology dynamics and  function  i also would like to place this in the  context of neuron development on  morphogenesis  as vividly illustrated by this image of  differentiating stem cells  neurons are some of the morphologically  most complex cells in our bodies  and microtubules play a critical role in  generating this complexity  for example this image shows the outline  of a very young cortical neuron  differentiating from a human-induced  pluripotent stem cell  as you can easily see microtubules shown  here in white  invade every single one of the nascent  growth cones branching from this neurite  many neurodevelopmental disorders are  caused by proteins  related to the microtubule cytoskeleton  and in fact  there is now even a name for such  diseases that are caused by mutations in  tubulin itself  microtubules are cylindrical hollow  polymers of alpha-beta tubulin dimers  that are arranged head to tail in  protofilaments  in most mammalian cells 13  protofilaments are arranged in a  staggered helix to make up the  microtubule wall  gtp-loaded tubulin dimers polymerize  onto the microtubule plus end  and gtp is subsequently hydrolyzed in  the microtubule  wall gtp hydrolysis is associated with a  conformational change  longitudinally along tubulin  protofilaments moving adjacent tubulin  dimers closer together  gtp and gdpi loaded tubulin subunits  are thought to protect the growing  microtubule plus end from sudden  depolymerization  the structure of this protective cap is  complicated  and still not completely understood but  recent cryoelectron tomography data  have challenged older ideas in the field  and now strongly suggest  that microtubules grow as individual  curved protofilaments  that subsequently close into a tube  cells control microtubule polymerization  dynamics  and microtubule network organization on  many levels  for example c-cap5 or x-map-215  is a microtubule polymerase that  facilitates the addition of gtp  tubulin dimers to growing protofilament  ends and by itself  c-cap5 accelerates polymerization of  pure tubulin  by 5 to 10-fold  other proteins specifically recognize  the microtubule wall conformation  associated with the tubulin guanosine  nucleotide state  and binding proteins or ebs are thought  to bind to the gdpi tubulin conformation  near the growing microtubule end while  the neuronal microtubule associated  protein double cortine  recognizes the gdp microtubule lattice  this difference in specificity is shown  here in a cell expressing  m cherry tag eb-1 displaying the  characteristic comets at growing  microtubule ends in purple  and gfp-tagged double cortine in green  that binds along gdp microtubules and  does not overlap with eb-1 comets  double cortine of course is well known  for its function in brain development  as a multitude of double quartet  mutations can cause this encephalitis  spectrum diseases in which new neurons  do not properly migrate through the  developing cortex  however the molecular function of double  quartet in developing neurons is not  well understood  a few years ago we found that double  cortine specifically recognizes  straight gdp microtubules and our more  recent work in neurons indicates  that double corten is only present on  straight microtubules in growth cones  we hypothesize that double quarter  mediated mechanical stiffening of these  microtubules  may be important for growth cone advance  but this is a different story for  another day  instead i want to now focus on our  efforts to control protein complexes  at the end of growing microtubules that  depend on plastic protein interactions  with eb1  eb1 is a dimer with c-terminal carponin  homology domains  that recognize the growing microtubule  end structure  the n-terminal eb homology domain has a  hydrophobic pocket  that binds disordered protein regions  containing sxip  peptide motives that are also surrounded  by positive charges  there are about 40 such so-called plus  tips and mammalian cells  with diverse and often poorly  characterized functions  that are recruited to growing  microtubule ends through interactions  with eb1  because in cells microtubules grow on  the order of 20 micrometers per minute  we wanted to develop an optogenetic tool  to control plastic protein networks  at growing microtubule ends with high  spatial and temporal accuracy  while there are several plant and  microbial photoreceptors  that can be harnessed for optogenetic  approaches the love 2 domain of old  phototropin 1  that implants mediates blue light  phototropism has several advantages  the loft 2 domain is small only requires  a flavin mononucleotide cofactor  that is present in all eukaryotic cells  and reverts to the dark conformation  relatively quickly  upon absorption of a blue light photon  the major conformational change is an  undocking and unfolding of the j  alpha helix at the c terminus of the  loft domain  we further used an optogenetic system  pioneered by the han lab  in which a protein a z domain was  selected by phage display  to bind with high affinity to the loft 2  domain dark state  but this love2c dark complex rapidly  dissociates in blue light  this is seen in this proof of principle  experiment in which purified love 2 and  c dark  molecules only form a complex in the  dark but this complex is absent when we  run a native gel  while exposed to blue light  to generate a photoinactivated eb1  variant that we named pi eb1  and that could replace eb1's function as  a central plus tip complex organizer in  the dark  we inserted the love2c dark module  between the eb1n  and c-terminal domains with the  expectation  that blue light would result in  dissociation of the plus tip adapter  from the microtubule binding domain  resulting in disassembly of the plastic  complex at growing microtubule ends  this project was mostly worked by a  talented postdoc jeffrey  franharen who recently started his own  group at the erasmus medical center in  the netherlands  indeed in an experiment in which we  transiently expressed both halves of pi  eb1  tagged with different fluorescent  proteins blue light had no immediate  effect on the n-terminal domain  that directly binds to growing  microtubule ends in contrast the  c-terminal half  very quickly dissociated from growing  microtubule ends with a half-life of  less than 200 milliseconds  importantly the very rapid blue  light-induced pi ev1 dissociation  together with dark re-association  kinetics on the order of seconds  allows for subcellular control of plus  tip complex dissociation  with high spatial and temporal accuracy  this experiment of a cell expressing pi  ed1  with only the c terminal part tagged  with m cherry  illustrates this by alternating blue  light exposed regions  between the top and the bottom of the  cell  m cherry c dark eb1c rapidly  locally and reversibly dissociates from  growing microtubule ends  only in these blue light exposed regions  with this graph  showing the blue light illumination  profile and relative eb1c  plus and association when the top half  of the cell is exposed to blue light  and this graph showing on and off  switching of eb1 c  plus and binding in the two alternating  regions over time  on the other hand if we tag the n  terminal half of pi eb1 with m  cherry we can use this to analyze the  effect of pi eb1 dissociation on  microtubule polymerization dynamics  this movie shows 20 second time-lapse  sequences  of the same cell before during  and a few minutes after blue light  exposure demonstrating reversible  inhibition of microtubule growth  as indicated by a substantial decrease  of the microtubule growth rate  the duration of growth events and the  overall number of growing microtubules  lastly it is important to note that pi  eb1 is not dominant negative  and that in this and subsequent  experiments endogenous eb1 is removed  by either rna interference or crispr  genome editing and that in this case  all other plus tips that we have tested  also dissociate from microtubule ends  this brings up the question of how cells  achieve physiological microtubule growth  rates  in vitro purified tubulin polymerizes  much slower than microtubules growing  cells  and as i mentioned earlier while the  microtubule polymerase c-cap5 can  accelerate microtubule growth  by itself it is not sufficient to  support microtubule growth rates  typically seen in mammalian cells  however because there is published  evidence that there are interactions  between c-cap5  and the plastic protein network we next  tested fccap5 localization to growing  microtubule ends  dependent on eb1  however this experiment shows that in  contrast to ev1 dependent plus tips  which dissociate from growing  microtubule ends as a result of pi eb1  photodissociation  the dynamics of c-cap5 in cells appear  unchanged  this is further supported by our  observation that c-cap5 remains on  microtubule ends even in cells  in which both eb1 and eb3 have been  deleted by crispr genome editing  in addition microtubule associated c-cap  5 looks very different and much more  punctated  compared with typical eb1 comets and in  data not shown here  c-cap 5 localization correlates very  poorly with eb1 on growing microtubule  ends  these data and the current model that  microtubules grow as curved  protofilaments  prompted us to think that two different  structural transitions  on growing microtubule ends must occur  together  for optimal microtubule growth in cells  by itself  tubulin polymerizes slowly at rates of  one to two micrometers per minute  while c-cap5 catalyzed protofilament  elongation  can increase the microtubule growth rate  microtubules can only grow as  quickly as protofilaments close into a  tube and the formation of protofilament  lateral interactions  becomes rate limiting eb1 on the other  hand  binds between protofilaments and may  promote protofilament zippering but does  not increase the rate of tubulin dimer  addition  only if both structural changes are  optimally coordinated  microtubules grow at the high rates  exceeding 20 micrometers per minute  observed in mammalian cells  i have just shown you that pi b1  photodissociation inhibits microtubule  growth  but how does this affect the microtubule  network as a whole  in this pi b1 cell microtubules are  labeled with m cherry tubulin  blue light exposure is globally turned  on when the image on the left appears  that shows the gfp tag c terminal half  of pi b1  comets at the microtubule ends can be  briefly seen before pi b1 dissociates  soon after the blue light is turned on  microtubules begin to shorten and  disappear from the cell periphery  note that not all microtubules are  affected equally possibly indicating  microtubule populations with different  stabilities  while it is difficult to determine the  amount of polymerized microtubules in a  cell  it is quite easy to measure  unpolymerized tubulin fluorescence  and the increase of unpolymerized  tubulin in this graph  shows that the microtubule network  reaches a new equilibrium  within a minute  this is even more evident in a cell  expressing dominant active rac1  a small row gtpase that induces cell  edge actin polymerization and retrograde  flow  microtubules are rapidly cleared from  the right side of the cell  when it is exposed to blue light and  this induced microtubule network  polarity  can be maintained for an extended period  of time  although it is known that microtubules  polarize in most migrating cell types  such that the microtubule organizing  center and the bulk of microtubule  growth  are oriented toward the direction of  migration it is less well understood  if this microtubule network  reorientation is a cause or a  consequence of directed migration  we therefore used our pi eb1 cells to  address this question  the blue square in this video is not a  physical barrier  but simply an image of the reflected  blue light pattern  nevertheless this pi eb1 cell turns  around every time it enters a blue light  zone  and remains trapped within this virtual  box for more than 8 hours  this and other experiments indicate that  eb  dependent plus dip networks are required  for directional movement  and i invite you to read our paper for  more detail  the pi eb1 experiments that i have shown  up to this point  were all in a cancer cell line and  relied on knockout of the endogenous eb1  gene  and plasmid-based expression of pi ev1  constructs  to adapt this to more complicated cell  systems  such as developing neurons we came up  with a genome editing strategy  to insert a bicystronic genetic cassette  containing the love2c dark module  directly into the eb1 gene  and thus in one step convert eb1 into a  light sensitive variant  genomic pcr and immunoblotting simply  confirm successful insertion of this pi  element  and these images show induced  pluripotent stem cell colonies  generated in this manner in the dark and  during blue light exposure  demonstrating the expected pi eb1  dissociation at growing microtubule ends  the final experiment i want to show  today is work in progress  coming back to our initial question how  plastic protein complexes control  neuron morphogenesis this video  shows the extending neurite of a newborn  cortical neuron  differentiating from pi ed1 induced  pluripotent stem cells  the neurite growth cone advances along a  micro patterned laminine track  toward a blue light box  i will let you make your own conclusions  here but would also like to caution  that this is currently one of few  experiments and we are currently working  to combine such optogenetic assays with  chemotactic gradients  to test how growth cone guidance is  controlled by microtubule plus and  protein interactions  all that remains now is to thank our  funders the people who are doing the  work  and you for listening i'm happy to  receive feedback or answer questions  either by email  or on our twitter account thank you

